  "hints": [
    "Membership tests are the whole problem. Which structure makes 'is this value in a?' O(1)?",
    "Build a set from one array, filter the other array's distinct values through it, then sort the result."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "python": "def shared_values(a, b):\n    return sorted(set(a) & set(b))\n",
    "javascript": "export function sharedValues(a, b) {\n  const setA = new Set(a);\n  const shared = [...new Set(b)].filter((v) => setA.has(v));\n  return shared.sort((x, y) => x - y);\n}\n"
  },
  "solutionExplanation": "Convert both arrays to sets and intersect: set membership is O(1), so the intersection is O(n + m), and the final sort is on the (usually much smaller) shared set. Watch the JS pitfall: Array.prototype.sort() compares as strings by default \u2014 pass a numeric comparator."
